# Movie Recommendation System

## Elevvo Pathways – Machine Learning Track
**Level 2 | Task 5: Movie Recommendation System**

## 1. Project Overview

This project develops and evaluates a movie recommendation system using collaborative filtering and matrix factorization techniques.

The system analyses user–movie rating patterns to recommend movies that users may enjoy. Four recommendation approaches were implemented and evaluated using the MovieLens 100K dataset.

The main objective is to explore different recommendation techniques and compare their performance using Precision@K.

## 2. Objectives

- Build a user-item rating matrix.
- Implement user-based collaborative filtering.
- Calculate similarity between users using cosine similarity.
- Implement item-based collaborative filtering.
- Apply Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) for matrix factorization.
- Generate personalised movie recommendations.
- Evaluate recommendation performance using Precision@K.
- Compare the performance of the implemented models.

## 3. Dataset

**Dataset:** MovieLens 100K

**Source:** GroupLens Research

[Download MovieLens 100K Dataset](https://files.grouplens.org/datasets/movielens/ml-100k.zip)

The dataset contains:
- 100,000 movie ratings.
- 943 users.
- 1,682 movies.
- Ratings ranging from 1 to 5.

For model development and evaluation, the official MovieLens split was used:

| Dataset | Number of ratings | Percentage |
|---|---:|---:|
| Training (`u1.base`) | 80,000 | 80% |
| Testing (`u1.test`) | 20,000 | 20% |

## 4. Technologies and Libraries

- Python
- Google Colab
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Scikit-learn
- Cosine Similarity
- Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)

## 5. Methodology

### 5.1 Data Exploration and Preprocessing

The dataset was loaded and explored to understand its structure, rating distribution, user activity and movie popularity.

A user-item matrix was constructed, with users represented as rows and movies as columns. Missing ratings were filled with zero for the baseline recommendation implementations.

### 5.2 User-Based Collaborative Filtering

Cosine similarity was used to identify users with similar movie-rating patterns.

The 20 most similar users were selected as neighbours. Movies highly rated by these neighbours but not previously rated by the target user were considered for recommendation.

### 5.3 Item-Based Collaborative Filtering

Cosine similarity was calculated between movies using their user-rating patterns.

Two implementations were explored:

- Original item-based approach using similarity-weighted predictions.
- Improved item-based approach using the 20 most similar rated movies when estimating candidate movie ratings.

A minimum popularity threshold of 20 training ratings was also applied to reduce the influence of sparsely rated movies.

### 5.4 Matrix Factorization Using SVD

Truncated Singular Value Decomposition was implemented using Scikit-learn.

The model used 50 latent factors to represent underlying user preferences and movie characteristics.

The learned user and movie factor representations were combined to reconstruct predicted ratings.

## 6. Evaluation Method

The models were evaluated using Precision@K.

A movie was considered relevant when its actual rating in the test dataset was 4 or 5.

The evaluation compared recommended movies against relevant movies in the held-out test dataset.

**Precision@K formula:**

Precision@K = Relevant recommended movies in top K / K

For the main model comparison, Precision@10 was used.

The evaluation covered 456 users who met the eligibility conditions, including having relevant test ratings and sufficient candidate recommendations.

## 7. Experimental Results

### Final Model Comparison

| Recommendation Model | Precision@10 |
|---|---:|
| User-Based Collaborative Filtering | **35.61%** |
| SVD Matrix Factorization | **33.00%** |
| Improved Item-Based Collaborative Filtering | **8.44%** |
| Original Item-Based Collaborative Filtering | **2.43%** |

### Additional User-Based Evaluation

| Evaluation Metric | Precision |
|---|---:|
| Precision@5 | 40.96% |
| Precision@10 | 35.61% |
| Precision@20 | 29.08% |

### Key Findings

1. User-based collaborative filtering achieved the highest Precision@10 of 35.61%.
2. SVD achieved 33.00%, demonstrating competitive recommendation performance.
3. The improved item-based implementation increased Precision@10 from 2.43% to 8.44%.
4. User-based Precision decreased as the recommendation list increased from 5 to 20 movies.
5. The results demonstrate that recommendation performance depends on the selected algorithm and implementation.

